WEBVTT

00:00:00.386 --> 00:00:03.511
A defence-in-depth security & intelligence

00:00:03.557 --> 00:00:06.798
strategy - that just happens to be a

00:00:06.861 --> 00:00:09.328
creative production platform

00:00:10.212 --> 00:00:12.950
The zero-trust organizational immune

00:00:13.032 --> 00:00:16.091
system that wraps around what you already

00:00:16.172 --> 00:00:19.288
do - so the routine creative work your

00:00:19.349 --> 00:00:22.322
teams need every day happens inside your

00:00:22.383 --> 00:00:25.845
perimeter instead of leaking out of it.

00:00:26.629 --> 00:00:29.169
What's in it for you.

00:00:29.942 --> 00:00:32.800
You get to be the person who said yes to

00:00:32.862 --> 00:00:35.645
something both safe and popular.

00:00:36.425 --> 00:00:39.138
You close an exfiltration hole, gain

00:00:39.199 --> 00:00:42.731
capability, and delete a request queue in

00:00:42.792 --> 00:00:45.241
one move - the rare security win that

00:00:45.291 --> 00:00:47.870
makes you more liked, not less.

00:00:48.654 --> 00:00:52.151
No 3am call from legal because embargoed

00:00:52.221 --> 00:00:54.877
files or customer data found their way

00:00:54.929 --> 00:00:58.075
into a random web tool; fewer SaaS

00:00:58.135 --> 00:01:00.880
vendors, contracts, and audits on your

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:03.816
plate; and a fully reproducible audit

00:01:03.875 --> 00:01:07.245
trail you can point to when someone asks.

00:01:08.023 --> 00:01:10.980
You sleep better, and brighten a few days

00:01:11.043 --> 00:01:12.319
doing it.

00:01:13.123 --> 00:01:16.853
Lolly is no second-class creative tool: it

00:01:16.915 --> 00:01:19.169
puts production-quality output in

00:01:19.231 --> 00:01:22.061
everyone's hands, and the brand-guided

00:01:22.122 --> 00:01:25.544
creation experience is second to none.

00:01:26.326 --> 00:01:29.635
The reason it is safe to hand out widely

00:01:29.699 --> 00:01:33.115
is architectural: nothing uploads that you

00:01:33.177 --> 00:01:35.672
didn't put there, every result is

00:01:35.733 --> 00:01:39.026
reproducible, and every export can carry

00:01:39.129 --> 00:01:41.553
multiple layers of industry-leading

00:01:41.615 --> 00:01:43.769
cryptographic records.

00:01:44.561 --> 00:01:47.053
No matter how a document reached your

00:01:47.113 --> 00:01:50.070
desk, you can see its full provenance,

00:01:50.129 --> 00:01:52.929
whether it has been tampered with, and

00:01:52.981 --> 00:01:56.520
whether you can recreate it pixel-perfect.

00:01:57.311 --> 00:01:59.943
Where it stands today.

00:02:00.739 --> 00:02:03.687
Lolly's security properties are strong by

00:02:03.748 --> 00:02:06.828
design, and its cryptography and

00:02:06.922 --> 00:02:09.590
file-parsing engines are going through

00:02:09.727 --> 00:02:12.037
SUSE's enterprise-grade infrastructure

00:02:12.098 --> 00:02:13.344
hardening.

00:02:14.115 --> 00:02:16.771
The seals, on-device signing, and

00:02:16.831 --> 00:02:19.640
encryption below are real and defensible

00:02:19.700 --> 00:02:22.766
now, and maturing toward independent

00:02:22.827 --> 00:02:25.972
certification - so where a contract calls

00:02:26.054 --> 00:02:29.277
for certified assurance, deploy them as

00:02:29.338 --> 00:02:31.551
defence-in-depth while that process

00:02:31.612 --> 00:02:32.895
completes.

00:02:34.039 --> 00:02:36.787
The strategic advantage

00:02:37.758 --> 00:02:40.610
The usual way routine creative work gets

00:02:40.672 --> 00:02:44.583
done is a liability surface: files emailed

00:02:44.678 --> 00:02:47.678
to external design contractors, brand

00:02:47.752 --> 00:02:51.586
assets uploaded to a dozen SaaS editors,

00:02:51.650 --> 00:02:54.560
customer data pasted into a stranger's web

00:02:54.620 --> 00:02:57.820
tool to "just make a quick graphic."

00:02:58.596 --> 00:03:01.533
Every one of those is data leaving your

00:03:01.596 --> 00:03:02.944
control.

00:03:03.744 --> 00:03:06.069
Lolly inverts it.

00:03:06.836 --> 00:03:08.859
The work that drove those leaks - the

00:03:08.919 --> 00:03:11.691
quote card, the localized banner, the

00:03:11.750 --> 00:03:14.990
event badge, the redacted screenshot - now

00:03:15.050 --> 00:03:17.272
happens on a tool that runs on the

00:03:17.331 --> 00:03:20.750
employee's own device, against your brand,

00:03:20.799 --> 00:03:23.020
with no server in the loop.

00:03:23.799 --> 00:03:27.007
You didn't add a control on top of a risky

00:03:27.068 --> 00:03:30.348
workflow; you replaced the risky workflow

00:03:30.413 --> 00:03:33.454
with one that has no exfiltration path to

00:03:33.515 --> 00:03:34.944
begin with.

00:03:35.325 --> 00:03:38.825
Configuration is yours.

00:03:39.175 --> 00:03:45.075
The engine and shells are open source «MPL-2 point 0».

00:03:45.425 --> 00:03:56.950
Overlay your own auth, telemetry, or CA; host it or don't; you hold full feature and cost control, git-tracked, not locked in a SaaS database.

00:03:57.694 --> 00:04:01.419
Governance can be data, not a dashboard.

00:04:02.191 --> 00:04:04.923
When you want that control, manage the

00:04:05.017 --> 00:04:07.985
tool catalog as a Git repository -

00:04:08.048 --> 00:04:10.137
pull-request review becomes brand

00:04:10.174 --> 00:04:13.322
approval, with a full audit trail and

00:04:13.416 --> 00:04:16.031
instant rollback of every template your

00:04:16.092 --> 00:04:18.169
workforce can touch.

00:04:18.915 --> 00:04:22.100
It's an option, not an obligation, and it

00:04:22.161 --> 00:04:25.425
lands on exactly one desk: creators work

00:04:25.480 --> 00:04:28.532
entirely in-app, saving what they make as

00:04:28.581 --> 00:04:31.374
a session and passing it on as a share

00:04:31.434 --> 00:04:34.606
link, a backup or a live collaboration -

00:04:34.681 --> 00:04:36.770
none of that needs git.

00:04:37.550 --> 00:04:40.138
When one of those sessions deserves to

00:04:40.198 --> 00:04:43.306
become a permanent starting point, whoever

00:04:43.397 --> 00:04:46.379
runs the deployment opens the link,

00:04:46.456 --> 00:04:49.600
records its values as a template on that

00:04:49.667 --> 00:04:52.944
tool in the brand pack, and commits.

00:04:53.726 --> 00:04:56.396
From then on it shows up in the tool's

00:04:56.474 --> 00:04:58.865
"New from template" chooser and is

00:04:58.951 --> 00:05:00.769
deep-linkable as ?

00:05:01.545 --> 00:05:03.643
template= .

00:05:04.410 --> 00:05:07.117
Git is the admin's locking step, used

00:05:07.177 --> 00:05:10.251
once, and never something a creator has to

00:05:10.314 --> 00:05:11.369
touch.

00:05:12.163 --> 00:05:15.044
See Adoption & Governance.

00:05:15.828 --> 00:05:18.594
Guard-rails are structural.

00:05:19.354 --> 00:05:21.889
Brand constraints are hard-coded into

00:05:21.951 --> 00:05:24.839
templates, not published as guidelines

00:05:24.900 --> 00:05:26.769
people can ignore.

00:05:27.561 --> 00:05:30.769
The wrong output isn't discouraged - it's

00:05:30.862 --> 00:05:32.693
unrepresentable.

00:05:33.490 --> 00:05:36.294
You govern the whole relay.

00:05:37.054 --> 00:05:39.605
A creative authors the rules and a

00:05:39.662 --> 00:05:42.153
developer scales them, but it's the

00:05:42.225 --> 00:05:45.209
operator who makes that lifecycle safe to

00:05:45.270 --> 00:05:48.204
run org-wide - the same tool that lets a

00:05:48.264 --> 00:05:51.078
rep self-serve on a plane is one you can

00:05:51.144 --> 00:05:53.745
gate through Git review, deploy through

00:05:53.829 --> 00:05:57.570
your MDM and verify cryptographically.

00:05:58.361 --> 00:06:00.638
See how the roles compound in The

00:06:00.700 --> 00:06:04.009
lifecycle of a campaign, and how you

00:06:04.073 --> 00:06:07.094
govern it in Adoption & Governance.

00:06:08.214 --> 00:06:10.353
Delete the request queue while

00:06:10.416 --> 00:06:12.669
proliferating content.

00:06:13.454 --> 00:06:15.941
One goal of Lolly is design-request

00:06:15.984 --> 00:06:19.161
deflection: routine requests that never

00:06:19.222 --> 00:06:21.967
need to reach a designer because the

00:06:22.040 --> 00:06:24.481
person who needed the asset made it

00:06:24.537 --> 00:06:27.694
themselves, correctly, in minutes.

00:06:28.459 --> 00:06:30.877
Every deflected ticket is both a

00:06:30.938 --> 00:06:33.754
productivity win and one fewer file

00:06:33.815 --> 00:06:35.544
changing hands.

00:06:36.335 --> 00:06:38.501
Lolly is built to fit how your

00:06:38.566 --> 00:06:41.574
organisation actually operates - there's

00:06:41.651 --> 00:06:44.819
no single right way to deploy it:

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:48.025
Deploy, don't serve.

00:06:48.375 --> 00:06:54.950
Ship Lolly to devices through your existing MDM «Intune, Jamf, Munki…»

00:06:55.300 --> 00:07:12.750
It runs locally as a desktop/mobile app or an offline PWA - works behind any firewall, in any air-gapped environment, with no server to maintain and IT in control of the update cadence.

00:07:13.100 --> 00:07:15.300
Serve only.

00:07:15.650 --> 00:07:25.275
Run one instance inside your network «or behind a VPN»; users reach it in a browser, nothing installed.

00:07:25.625 --> 00:07:33.775
Publish a tool once, everyone has it immediately; pair with your IdP for access control.

00:07:34.519 --> 00:07:35.843
Hybrid.

00:07:36.633 --> 00:07:39.952
Local apps for offline field work, an

00:07:40.053 --> 00:07:42.324
always-current browser version for

00:07:42.387 --> 00:07:44.878
borrowed machines - both pointed at the

00:07:44.972 --> 00:07:46.944
same tool library.

00:07:47.709 --> 00:07:50.720
The full deploy models and administration

00:07:50.803 --> 00:07:53.279
walkthrough live in Deployment and

00:07:53.339 --> 00:07:55.019
Configuration.

00:07:56.156 --> 00:07:59.326
Anti-exfiltration utilities

00:08:00.255 --> 00:08:03.108
A category of Lolly tools - the privacy

00:08:03.169 --> 00:08:06.604
utilities - exists specifically to keep

00:08:06.667 --> 00:08:09.344
files inside the perimeter.

00:08:10.122 --> 00:08:12.105
Strip hidden data

00:08:13.143 --> 00:08:16.707
Remove location and all hidden identifying

00:08:16.738 --> 00:08:19.379
information from documents and media

00:08:19.460 --> 00:08:20.669
files.

00:08:21.449 --> 00:08:22.820
Text Helper

00:08:23.772 --> 00:08:27.166
Anonymize, encode, format, and manipulate

00:08:27.228 --> 00:08:30.144
structured and unstructured text.

00:08:30.910 --> 00:08:32.911
Compress PDF

00:08:33.916 --> 00:08:37.429
Shrink an oversized PDF on-device, so

00:08:37.493 --> 00:08:40.539
nobody reaches for a third-party "compress

00:08:40.599 --> 00:08:44.442
my PDF" website the moment a file is too

00:08:44.513 --> 00:08:47.624
big to email - which is exactly where data

00:08:47.715 --> 00:08:49.620
slips out the window.

00:08:50.388 --> 00:08:53.494
All of these are on-device transforms:

00:08:53.559 --> 00:08:56.393
your file or data goes in, cleaned bytes

00:08:56.454 --> 00:08:59.478
come out, and there is no server to upload

00:08:59.568 --> 00:09:00.545
to.

00:09:01.311 --> 00:09:03.594
They are the deliberate opposite of the

00:09:03.682 --> 00:09:07.397
typical "upload your file to a stranger's

00:09:07.457 --> 00:09:10.091
website to clean it" tool that a

00:09:10.154 --> 00:09:12.268
well-meaning employee reaches for

00:09:12.327 --> 00:09:13.694
otherwise.

00:09:14.459 --> 00:09:17.676
Text Helper is the same bargain for text

00:09:17.740 --> 00:09:19.744
rather than files.

00:09:20.503 --> 00:09:23.448
It is the tabbed workbench an employee

00:09:23.511 --> 00:09:25.861
would otherwise go looking for on a

00:09:25.926 --> 00:09:29.577
stranger's site, and it declares no inputs

00:09:29.643 --> 00:09:32.213
at all because nothing it touches ever

00:09:32.274 --> 00:09:34.094
leaves the page.

00:09:34.851 --> 00:09:38.043
Compress PDF completes the set: the

00:09:38.122 --> 00:09:40.686
oversized attachment shrinks under a

00:09:40.747 --> 00:09:43.905
quality setting you choose, on the machine

00:09:43.961 --> 00:09:46.019
that already holds it.

00:09:47.144 --> 00:09:50.614
Determinism & reproducibility

00:09:51.506 --> 00:09:55.095
Every tool input is expressible as a URL

00:09:55.156 --> 00:09:58.281
parameter, and the same inputs produce the

00:09:58.360 --> 00:09:59.869
same file.

00:10:00.654 --> 00:10:04.318
That has two operator consequences:

00:10:05.104 --> 00:10:08.518
A URL is the artifact.

00:10:09.268 --> 00:10:12.073
Commit the link, regenerate the asset on

00:10:12.127 --> 00:10:15.371
demand - no binaries checked into Git, no

00:10:15.431 --> 00:10:18.670
chasing "the latest version" in chat.

00:10:19.444 --> 00:10:22.209
Asset and tool ID'S are permanent

00:10:22.271 --> 00:10:25.538
contracts, so a link minted today still

00:10:25.597 --> 00:10:27.294
resolves later.

00:10:28.056 --> 00:10:30.741
The CLI is the same render path as the

00:10:30.820 --> 00:10:34.229
GUI, so build pipelines and the app never

00:10:34.310 --> 00:10:35.394
drift.

00:10:36.159 --> 00:10:39.663
Generate OG images, social cards, and data

00:10:39.724 --> 00:10:43.120
visuals at build time, reproducibly.

00:10:43.874 --> 00:10:46.949
Prompt to Image is determinism at its

00:10:47.021 --> 00:10:50.393
plainest: the text is the whole input, the

00:10:50.465 --> 00:10:53.582
typeset image is the whole output, and the

00:10:53.672 --> 00:10:56.895
same text always sets the same way.

00:10:57.994 --> 00:11:01.198
Provenance & Content Credentials

00:11:02.127 --> 00:11:05.359
Exports can carry Content Credentials - a

00:11:05.452 --> 00:11:09.046
signed C2PA manifest bound to a hash of

00:11:09.094 --> 00:11:10.920
the file's bytes.

00:11:11.686 --> 00:11:14.386
Any later change to the file breaks the

00:11:14.491 --> 00:11:18.596
seal, so a C2PA-aware verifier detects

00:11:18.652 --> 00:11:21.969
alteration cryptographically, offline.

00:11:22.743 --> 00:11:26.361
The credential is tamper-evident: it flags

00:11:26.448 --> 00:11:29.529
tampering rather than preventing it, which

00:11:29.619 --> 00:11:32.410
is precisely what makes fully offline

00:11:32.472 --> 00:11:34.594
verification possible.

00:11:34.975 --> 00:11:38.250
On by default, on-device.

00:11:38.600 --> 00:11:53.500
The signing key is generated on the device, is non-extractable «even Lolly can't read it», and signing happens locally - only optional identity enrolment ever touches the network.

00:11:53.850 --> 00:11:56.100
Trust tiers.

00:11:56.450 --> 00:12:03.275
An un-enrolled export is structurally valid but signed anonymously «untrusted».

00:12:03.625 --> 00:12:17.100
Enrol a verified identity «short-lived certificate from the Lolly CA, tied to an email» and verifiers pinning the Lolly root report trusted + the signer's email.

00:12:17.450 --> 00:12:26.275
A trusted timestamp authority and third-party-validator green «C2PA conformance» are on the roadmap.

00:12:26.625 --> 00:12:33.700
Every tier is explicit, and a file only ever claims the trust it can prove.

00:12:34.430 --> 00:12:38.045
Credential lifetime is the operator/user's

00:12:38.111 --> 00:12:42.595
call at signing time: 7 / 30 / 90

00:12:42.674 --> 00:12:47.269
/ 365 days, default 30.

00:12:47.650 --> 00:12:50.350
The Lolly Imprint.

00:12:50.700 --> 00:13:08.975
A second, complementary signal that is on by default: an invisible pixel watermark baked into raster exports «and the Lolly-rendered rasters inside a PDF/PPTX, never a user's own embedded image».

00:13:09.325 --> 00:13:23.825
Where the credential dies to any container change, the Imprint survives a re-save or screenshot - a durable "these pixels passed through Lolly" hint, presence-only, no personal data.

00:13:24.175 --> 00:13:33.900
It is security-through-obscurity, not a hardened defence, and complements the credential rather than replacing it.

00:13:34.250 --> 00:13:38.000
imprint=0 opts out.

00:13:38.350 --> 00:13:42.350
Durable Content Credentials «opt-in».

00:13:42.700 --> 00:14:04.375
A raster export can additionally carry an invisible durable mark that encodes a soft-binding identifier, so the C2PA credential can be recovered even after a social upload or re-save has stripped the file's metadata - the case where a normal credential would be lost.

00:14:04.725 --> 00:14:14.575
It is raster-only and costs a neural-encode pass, so it is off by default «durable=1 to turn it on».

00:14:14.925 --> 00:14:24.950
Lolly recognises its own durable mark offline on /verify today; recovery by third-party tools «e-g.

00:14:25.300 --> 00:14:31.725
Adobe» follows once the industry soft-binding resolution is in place.

00:14:32.075 --> 00:14:35.625
Verification is on-device.

00:14:35.975 --> 00:14:47.725
Drop any file on /verify «or lolly validate » for an offline report of whether it was genuinely made with Lolly and unchanged since.

00:14:48.075 --> 00:15:16.425
The web Verify view also flags AI-generated content, detects the Lolly Imprint, verifies SEAL signatures «a byte-level signature - with zero network requests: the engine takes an injected DNS key resolver and no shell injects one today, so a record carrying its own inline pk= key verifies fully offline while a DNS-keyed one reports "no key resolver and no inline key" rather than reaching out - see

00:15:16.775 --> 00:15:34.925
SealPublicKeyResolver in engine/src/seal-ts», optionally deep-scans for third-party pixel watermarks «a one-time on-device model download», and surfaces hidden data - all without uploading the file.

00:15:35.275 --> 00:15:39.250
See Content Credentials Identity.

00:15:39.600 --> 00:15:42.700
Interoperability notes.

00:15:43.050 --> 00:15:55.300
Lolly verifies its own credentials and many third-party ones offline today, including reading C2PA claim v2 manifests from other producers.

00:15:55.650 --> 00:16:23.425
Two containers remain in progress, both because C2PA has no standardised mapping for them yet, so Lolly carries the credential in a place of its own and Lolly's verifier is the one that reads it back: WebM «the manifest rides as a Matroska attachment» and Ogg/Opus «a C2PA= field in the OpusTags comment header, with that byte range excluded from the binding so the audio still hashes identically».

00:16:23.775 --> 00:16:36.550
Everything else stamps to spec - third-party tools verify Lolly's MP4, M4A, MP3, WAV, PNG, JPEG and PDF out of the box.

00:16:36.900 --> 00:16:47.125
See engine/src/c2pa-containers-ts for both mappings; they converge on the standard once it settles.

00:16:48.230 --> 00:16:50.909
Encryption & passwording

00:16:51.796 --> 00:16:54.304
For files that must travel locked,

00:16:54.372 --> 00:16:56.920
everything happens on-device:

00:16:57.300 --> 00:17:15.325
PDF open-password - Standard is a 40-bit RC4 deterrent «opens anywhere, may travel in a link»; Strong is AES-256 «PDF 2 point 0», typed at export and never put in a link.

00:17:15.675 --> 00:17:29.975
Locked downloads - a ZIP, a Projects folder, or a batch run can be locked whole: Standard ZipCrypto «weak, universal» or Strong AES-256 «WinZip AE-2».

00:17:30.325 --> 00:17:43.100
Defence-in-depth: any PDF inside a Strong zip is also individually AES-256-locked, so it stays locked after unpacking.

00:17:43.823 --> 00:17:46.078
Password-gated share links - the whole

00:17:46.138 --> 00:17:50.872
link state is AES-256-encrypted under a

00:17:50.955 --> 00:17:55.045
PBKDF2-derived key; only ciphertext

00:17:55.099 --> 00:17:57.536
travels, the password is never in the

00:17:57.596 --> 00:18:00.043
link, and decryption happens in the

00:18:00.100 --> 00:18:02.020
recipient's browser.

00:18:03.145 --> 00:18:04.611
Air-gap ready

00:18:05.150 --> 00:18:15.050
Air-gap is a first-class deployment, not a special mode - Lolly runs with no network at render time out of the box.

00:18:15.400 --> 00:18:29.925
The web shell is an offline-first PWA «service worker»; fonts and WASM are stored on-device; tool state is persisted locally through the host bridge, never localStorage.

00:18:30.275 --> 00:18:44.675
The supported way for a tool to reach the network is an allowlisted host-net capability it declares in its manifest - a shell that can't «or won't» fulfil it stubs it out.

00:18:45.025 --> 00:19:01.400
That is a portability contract rather than an enforced boundary «see the hooks note below», which is why reviewing tool code stays the control - though on an air-gapped device there is nothing to reach either way.

00:19:01.750 --> 00:19:18.250
Ship the shells to devices through your MDM, or serve one instance inside your network, and a fully air-gapped install renders, exports, encrypts, and verifies credentials with nothing to phone home to.

00:19:19.339 --> 00:19:20.871
Good to know

00:19:21.851 --> 00:19:24.438
A few things worth having straight before

00:19:24.516 --> 00:19:26.370
you roll it out:

00:19:26.750 --> 00:19:29.725
Hardening in progress.

00:19:30.075 --> 00:19:44.875
The cryptography and parsers are going through SUSE's enterprise-scale hardening «see above» - strong by design today; deploy as defence-in-depth where a contract calls for certified assurance.

00:19:45.620 --> 00:19:50.144
*Tool hooks are not a security sandbox.

00:19:50.906 --> 00:19:54.014
A tool's optional hooks-js runs with the

00:19:54.075 --> 00:19:57.314
host bridge injected, but in a browser

00:19:57.409 --> 00:20:00.500
shell it executes in the page's realm and

00:20:00.561 --> 00:20:05.044
can* reach window/document/fetch.

00:20:05.820 --> 00:20:08.843
Treat tool code the way you treat any code

00:20:08.911 --> 00:20:10.893
you run - review it.

00:20:11.662 --> 00:20:14.575
This is why an org that runs a shared

00:20:14.636 --> 00:20:17.875
catalog can gate it through Git review;

00:20:17.949 --> 00:20:21.038
either way, run only tools you've reviewed

00:20:21.105 --> 00:20:23.819
until Worker isolation ships.

00:20:24.589 --> 00:20:28.318
Content Credentials are tamper-evident.

00:20:29.081 --> 00:20:32.194
They detect alteration rather than prevent

00:20:32.293 --> 00:20:35.944
it - see the interoperability notes above.

00:20:36.325 --> 00:20:39.275
Two encryption tiers.

00:20:39.625 --> 00:20:53.825
Standard locks are quick, universal deterrents; Strong «AES-256» is full protection - reach for Strong for anything sensitive, noting it wants a modern reader.

00:20:54.934 --> 00:20:56.997
Where to go next

00:20:58.034 --> 00:21:01.516
Security & Verification - the standards,

00:21:01.580 --> 00:21:04.417
primitives, trust model, and testing

00:21:04.474 --> 00:21:06.955
behind the credentials and encryption

00:21:07.021 --> 00:21:08.168
above.

00:21:08.949 --> 00:21:11.659
Adoption & Governance - personas, the

00:21:11.720 --> 00:21:14.803
deflection metric, and governance-as-data

00:21:14.868 --> 00:21:16.169
in full.

00:21:16.930 --> 00:21:21.457
Deployment - deploy/serve/hybrid, MDM, and

00:21:21.549 --> 00:21:23.970
self-hosting the services.

00:21:24.722 --> 00:21:27.773
Configuration - profiles, brand packs,

00:21:27.834 --> 00:21:31.044
capability gating, and feature flags.

00:21:31.788 --> 00:21:34.548
Privacy Policy - the formal statement of

00:21:34.609 --> 00:21:37.508
what is and isn't collected, stored, and

00:21:37.603 --> 00:21:38.619
sent.

00:21:39.000 --> 00:21:47.975
Server Surface - the complete inventory of what runs server-side «two optional components» versus on-device.
